--- body ---
## Context

`tickets/erg-github` is, in the README's own words (L104-105), "a separate
committed POSIX-sh helper (**it travels with the clone**)". Unlike `.ergrc`
and `AGENTS.md`, it is not an embedded asset: it is vendored into each
adopter repo as a plain committed file, and nothing in the toolchain ever
compares an adopter's copy against the shipped one.

That means it has **no staleness channel at all**. The asset machinery —
`.erg-assets`, `isCleanUpgrade`, `assetDriftWarnings`, the whole 3-state
dpkg compare that 0278, 0279 and 0281 exist to harden — does not cover this
file. An adopter running a year-old `erg-github` gets no warning, no drift
signal, and no `erg check` complaint.

The concrete bite: 0255 fixes a ticket-ID extraction gap in
`tickets/erg-github`'s `cmd_verify()`. 0255's own Reproduction section names
`aedist-technical-report` PR #442/#443 as where that bug actually fired —
and that repo has its own committed copy of the buggy script. Merging 0255
into git-erg fixes git-erg's copy and reaches nothing else. Every adopter
needs a manual re-vendor that nothing will ever prompt them to do.

Note the shape: this is the same defect class the asset-machinery tickets
are about — a tracked file drifting silently — applied to the one file none
of them covers. 0278's own invariant ("no tracked file is ever overwritten
or reverted without saying so") has a sibling this repo has never stated:
*no shipped file goes stale in an adopter without saying so.*

## Relevant files

- `tickets/erg-github` — the vendored helper itself
- `README.md` L100-108 — where the "travels with the clone" contract is stated
- `src/go/init.go` — `initAssetPaths`, `migrateAssetPaths`, `orphanAssetPaths`;
  `erg-github` appears in none of them
- `src/go/manifest.go` — the drift machinery that does not see this file
- `src/go/check.go` — where a staleness report would most naturally surface

## Actions

1. Decide the contract first, because the rest follows from it, and this is
   an architectural call, not an implementation detail. Three coherent
   options, and they are genuinely different products:
   a. **Make it an embedded asset** like `.ergrc`/`AGENTS.md` — `erg init`
      installs and upgrades it through the existing 3-state compare. Adopters
      converge automatically. Costs: the forge layer stops being optional,
      and a local edit to it now needs the same divergence handling.
   b. **Keep it vendored, add a version stamp** — `erg-github` carries a
      version line, `erg check` compares it against the running binary's
      expectation and reports staleness without touching the file. Cheapest
      honest fix: it makes the drift *visible* without claiming ownership of
      the file.
   c. **Declare it explicitly out of scope** — document in README that
      `erg-github` is a starting point adopters own outright, and that
      upstream fixes must be pulled manually. Costs nothing to build, but
      the cost lands on every adopter forever, silently.
2. Implement whichever the author picks.
3. Regardless of choice: record in 0255's wake which adopters are known to
   carry a stale copy. `aedist-technical-report` is one, confirmed by 0255's
   own reproduction.

## Test

If (a): the existing asset tests extend to cover `erg-github` — the same
fixtures 0281 builds.
If (b): a fixture repo with an old `erg-github` must produce a *visible*
staleness message from `erg check`, asserted on content, not exit code —
the same discipline 0281 uses for path A, because the defect here is also
silence.
If (c): a docs test asserting the README states the manual-pull contract.

## Decision (Action 1, settled 2026-09-16)

**Option (b): keep it vendored, make the drift visible.** erg ships a
read-only reference copy and reports divergence; it never writes the file.

Why not (a), full embedded-asset treatment: it makes the forge layer
load-bearing for `erg` core, which this ticket's own Invariants section
forbids, and it would give `erg init` a write path over a CI-adjacent script
the adopter may have customised.

Why not (c), document-only: the cost lands on every adopter forever, silently
— it is exactly the 0255 recurrence this ticket exists to close.

The shape of (b) as built, so a later reader does not have to reconstruct it:

- `src/go/assets/erg-github` is now the canonical copy and is embedded;
  `tickets/erg-github` is its deployment, pinned to it by `make regen-assets`
  and `tests/test_selfcoherence.sh`, exactly like `AGENTS.md`.
- `vendoredAssetPaths` (`src/go/init.go`) is a third path list, deliberately
  unreachable from every writer. It stays out of `initAssetPaths` because that
  list drives both `installAssets` (a write) and `buildManifest` (a stamp that
  would record an installation that never happened — 0292's defect).
- `vendoredDriftWarnings` (`src/go/manifest.go`) compares disk against embedded
  and emits a NOTE, appended OUTSIDE the managed branch: the managed side
  returns early with no manifest, and an unstamped store is precisely the
  long-unmaintained adopter most likely to be carrying a stale helper.
- The message claims no direction (older upstream copy vs. local
  customisation are indistinguishable by a hash compare), consults no stamp,
  and does not prescribe `erg init` — which does not touch a vendored path and
  would report success while changing nothing.

Durable record outside this ticket: `README.md` § "Forge layer: `erg-github`"
→ "Vendoring contract", and `erg check --help`.

## Known adopters carrying a stale copy (Action 3)

- `aedist-technical-report` — confirmed by 0255's own Reproduction section
  (PR #442/#443 are where the `cmd_verify()` ticket-ID extraction gap actually
  fired). Its committed copy predates the 0255 fix. Not edited from here: this
  ticket changes git-erg only, and the point of the fix is that the repo's own
  `erg check` now says so.

No other adopter was surveyed; the sweep is not the deliverable, the channel
is. Any adopter running an `erg` from this revision onward now discovers its
own answer.

## Verification

- [x] the contract is decided and written down somewhere durable, not just
      in this ticket — README § Vendoring contract, `erg check --help`, and
      the code comments on `vendoredAssetPaths` / `vendoredDriftWarnings`
- [x] an adopter carrying a stale `erg-github` either converges (a), is told
      (b), or is documented as owning the problem (c) — (b): told, on every
      `erg check`, with a manual re-vendor route
- [x] the known-stale adopters from Action 3 are listed — above

## Invariants

- `erg` core stays offline and forge-blind. Whatever is chosen here must not
  make the binary reach the network or depend on `gh`.
- A non-forge adopter is never wedged by this: `erg-github` is optional
  infrastructure and must remain so.

## Exit criteria

- The vendoring contract for `tickets/erg-github` is explicit, and drift in
  an adopter copy is either impossible, visible, or documented as the
  adopter's own responsibility

## Weighed and not built (PR #353 review, round 1)

Recorded because the ticket weighed (a)/(b)/(c) and never weighed these, and a
later reader should find the reasoning rather than re-derive it. Neither meets
the severity floor for a ticket of its own; if one bites in practice, it
arrives with evidence attached.

- **No acknowledge path.** An adopter who legitimately customises the file they
  are told they own gets the NOTE on every `erg check`, forever — a stronger
  nag than 0283's precedent, where a stamp lets a customised `.ergrc` stay
  quiet. Silencing it would mean erg recording a per-store fact about a file it
  does not own, which is the ownership claim this design declines to make. The
  message names the customisation case so a customiser can read it once.
- **The exec bit is invisible to the compare.** A copy with the right bytes and
  mode 0644 is silent, and then fails CI with "permission denied". Reporting
  mode is a second signal about a different property, on a bit some filesystems
  do not carry and for which no positive control was available; the README
  recipe keeps its `chmod +x` instead.

A CRLF checkout reporting as drift is not on this list: a CRLF copy of a
`/bin/sh` script is broken rather than differently encoded — whole-file
conversion (what `core.autocrlf` does) sends the kernel looking for an
interpreter named `/bin/sh\r` and nothing runs, a body-only CR lets the shell
start and die on the first mislexed command — so the report is correct either
way.

## Related

- 0255 — the fix whose reach this ticket is about. 0255 lands in git-erg's
  copy only; that is deliberate and does not wait on this ticket.
- 0278, 0281 — the same "a tracked file drifts silently" class, for the
  embedded assets that *do* have a staleness channel.
